Version 2: Recursion
Each Fibonacci number is the sum of the two before it, so the function can ask itself for those two:
def fib_recursive(n):
    if n == 1:          # base case 1: the 1st number is 0
        return 0
    if n == 2:          # base case 2: the 2nd number is 1
        return 1
    return fib_recursive(n - 1) + fib_recursive(n - 2)

This one has two base cases, because every call needs the previous two numbers, and the first two have nothing before them.


